6. AI Provider and API Configuration

The Super Admin panel must include a dedicated AI Settings page.

The system must not be hardcoded to one AI provider.

Use an integration layer compatible with OpenAI-style APIs where possible.

The Super Admin must be able to configure:

- API provider name
- API Base URL
- API Key
- Chat Model Name
- Embedding Model Name, if semantic search is enabled
- Request timeout
- Maximum response tokens
- Temperature or supported generation parameters
- Global AI enable/disable switch

Example configuration fields:

- "AI_BASE_URL"
- "AI_API_KEY"
- "AI_CHAT_MODEL"
- "AI_EMBEDDING_MODEL"

These are conceptual configuration names; the implementation may use a secure database-backed settings service instead of reading all settings directly from environment variables.

6.1 Secure Configuration

The Super Admin must be able to enter and update AI configuration through the UI.

Requirements:

- Never expose API keys to frontend JavaScript.
- Never return stored API keys in API responses.
- Encrypt stored API keys at rest.
- Provide masked key display.
- Allow secure key replacement.
- Never write API keys to logs.
- Support environment-variable bootstrap configuration when required.
- Provide a test connection button.
- Show connection and model availability status.
- Handle provider errors and timeouts gracefully.

6.2 Provider Compatibility

Use a provider adapter architecture.

The application should support OpenAI-compatible endpoints, allowing different providers and self-hosted model endpoints to be configured through the Base URL, API Key, and model name.

Do not hardcode provider-specific behavior into ticketing or Knowledge Base modules.

If an API provider does not support a required feature, handle that limitation explicitly rather than silently pretending the feature works.

6.3 AI Failure Handling

If the AI API is unavailable, times out, rejects a request, or returns an invalid response:

- Do not lose the customer's message.
- Do not display fabricated responses.
- Preserve the conversation.
- Display a clear fallback message.
- Offer human operator escalation.
- Log technical errors without logging private customer information or secrets.

Implement reasonable rate limits, request timeouts, retry policies, and usage controls.

