8. Shared File Hub

The second top-level workspace is an organization-wide file-sharing area.

It must function as a structured internal document library, not simply a list of uploaded files.

8.1 File Organization

Support:

- Department-based file areas.
- Folders and nested folders.
- Files and folders created by authorized operators.
- Folder names based on operator names or other administrator-defined structures.
- Search and filtering.
- File details and metadata.
- Upload date.
- Uploader information.
- File size.
- Download history where permitted.
- File versioning or safe replacement where appropriate.

The Super Admin must be able to manage the storage structure and configure storage limits.

8.2 Department Permissions

Implement strict department-level file access.

Default rules:

- Operators can upload and manage files in their authorized department.
- Operators can edit, rename, move, or delete files only when they have the appropriate permissions.
- Operators from other departments may view and download shared files according to the configured access policy.
- They cannot edit, replace, move, or delete files belonging to another department.
- Unauthorized users must not be able to access files by guessing direct file URLs.

The Super Admin can define exceptions through granular permissions.

8.3 Storage Management

The Super Admin must be able to:

- View total storage usage.
- View usage per department.
- View usage per operator.
- Set maximum storage capacity.
- Set department storage quotas.
- Set individual operator upload quotas if required.
- Configure allowed file types.
- Configure maximum upload size.
- View storage consumption and available capacity.
- Manage folders and files.
- Audit uploads, downloads, replacements, and deletions.

Store uploaded files outside the application container and outside the relational database.

Use a storage abstraction that supports local persistent storage initially and S3-compatible storage later.

