15. Critical Development Rules

- Do not create fake functionality to make the UI appear complete.
- Do not use hardcoded production credentials.
- Do not store sensitive secrets in source code.
- Do not use national ID as a primary key or expose it in URLs.
- Do not trust frontend permission checks.
- Do not expose private files through public static paths.
- Do not overwrite production data during deployment.
- Do not run destructive database initialization on every restart.
- Do not mix demo seed data with real production data.
- Demo data must only be inserted through an explicit development/demo process or when a verified empty database is initialized.
- Never automatically reseed or overwrite existing customer, ticket, operator, or file records.
- All database changes must use migrations.
- All important administrative and security actions must be auditable.
- Keep dependencies minimal and maintained.
- Keep modules isolated and understandable.
- Prefer established libraries over custom implementations of complex security features.
- Do not rewrite working architecture without a documented technical reason.
- Do not move to the next phase while critical tests in the current phase are failing.
- Never allow the AI to answer organizational questions using unsupported general model knowledge.
- Never allow department-specific AI retrieval to cross department security boundaries.
- Never lose customer messages when the AI service fails.
- Preserve the full conversation when handing off from AI to a human operator.

Before installing packages, verify their compatibility and install them through the project's package manager so that dependencies and lockfiles remain consistent. Production dependencies must be installed during the Docker image build; do not rely on running package installation manually inside a production container.

